 and applied in array order; the first error throws immediately without evaluating later operations
 or exposing partial changes.
 
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` applies JSON Merge Patch as defined by
+[RFC 7386](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386.html):
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+applyMergePatch({ name: "Ada", settings: { theme: "dark", old: true } }, {
+  settings: { theme: "light", old: null },
+}); // { name: "Ada", settings: { theme: "light" } }
+```
+
+Object patches merge recursively; null object members remove keys, including at nested levels.
+An object patch treats a non-object target as an empty object. Arrays and scalar patches replace
+the entire target, and a root null patch returns null. Arrays are replaced as a whole.
+The result is a deep copy without modifying or retaining references to either input.
+Both inputs follow the JSON requirements above, except that the target may also be `undefined`
+to represent an absent document. Invalid inputs throw errors.
+
 Run the tests with `npm test` (Node.js 22.18 or newer, with TypeScript support enabled).
